{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>Say, I pray you, you are my sister.</b> This sin that Abraham committed unwittingly is the one on account of which the Egyptian exile came about. It was certainly a great sin, for he brought his righteous wife into a stumbling block of iniquity because of his fear lest they kill him. He should have trusted in HaShem, may He be exalted, that He would deliver him and his wife. Moreover, he sinned also by leaving the Land concerning which he had been commanded from the outset, for he departed from there on account of the famine, and he should have trusted in HaShem, may He be exalted, that He would redeem him from death by famine. And on account of this sin the exile of Egypt was decreed upon his seed, for in the place of the sin, there is the punishment. This is the language of the Ramban, of blessed memory. And according to the opinion of our Rabbis of blessed memory, the Egyptian exile was on account of the sin of what he said, 'במה אדע כי אירשנה' — 'whereby shall I know that I shall inherit it,' for so they said in the Midrash: he said 'במה אדע,' and the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: 'ידוע תדע' — 'knowing you shall know.' And from this it appears that they counted it as a sin. Nevertheless, whether according to the opinion of our Rabbis of blessed memory or according to the opinion of the Rabbi [Ramban], all of it was a sin of speech, and on account of it they were punished with the Egyptian exile תמ״ג (430) years. And because sin is the cause of exile and punishment, it is divided into three parts — the parts of sin: the sin of thought, the sin of speech, and the sin of deed. Therefore the enlightened person should be roused concerning the end of this our exile, which is fitting to be three times longer than the exile of Egypt. For since they stood in Egypt תמ״ג (430) years on account of the sin of speech, how much the more so in the First Temple, where we served idolatry and sinned in all three parts of sin — in thought, in speech, and in deed — upon us were decreed תמ״ג (430) corresponding to speech, תמ״ג (430) corresponding to thought, תמ״ג (430) corresponding to deed. And this is the reckoning of Daniel (Daniel 12:12): 'אשרי המחכה ויגיע לימים אלף ומאתים ותשעים' — happy is he who waits and reaches one thousand two hundred and ninety days — which are three times תמ״ג (430). And therefore the prophet said (Micah 7:20): 'תתן אמ״ת ליעקב' — He swore to Jacob. He prayed concerning this end and said: may there be fulfilled one thousand two hundred and ninety to Jacob, that is, to the seed of Jacob. And if You do this with the attribute of truth — and if not, with Your attribute of lovingkindness which Abraham upheld, this is 'חסד לאברהם אשר נשבעת' — lovingkindness to Abraham which You swore, that is, there is an oath in the matter, for You swore to our fathers on the side of lovingkindness. And the oath is what is written (Deuteronomy 32:40): 'כי אשא אל שמים ידי' — 'For I lift up My hand to heaven,' and in Daniel it is written (Daniel 12:7): 'וישבע בחי העולם' — 'and he swore by Him who lives forever.'"], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The commentary cites Daniel 12:12 but the cited word אשרי המחכה ויגיע לימים אלף ומאתים ותשעים is actually Daniel 12:12 in most editions; verse reference retained as written. The number 1290 is stated explicitly in the text as three times 430 (תמ״ג = 40+40+3 = 443, but the text treats it as 430); rendered exactly as written without correction. The word אמ״ת in the Micah citation is spelled with a medial consonant in the Hebrew as cited; the verse reference and quoted phrase align with Micah 7:20."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:12:24.102365376Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}